WEBVTT

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:02.289
Last video ended with this question.

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:05.342
Six equal weights on a beam balance right

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:07.010
in the middle, at three point five.

00:00:07.694 --> 00:00:10.526
That's the expected value of one roll, but

00:00:10.547 --> 00:00:12.435
a die can never show three point five.

00:00:13.584 --> 00:00:14.915
Now a lopsided case.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.722
A vet clinic finds that a cat makes

00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:20.726
zero, one, two or three visits a year,

00:00:21.068 --> 00:00:23.991
with probabilities zero point one, zero point three,

00:00:24.373 --> 00:00:26.435
zero point four and zero point two.

00:00:27.222 --> 00:00:28.675
Where does this beam balance?

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.146
What does that number mean?

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.198
And how spread out are the visits?

00:00:32.735 --> 00:00:34.202
Here's what the balance point means.

00:00:34.806 --> 00:00:37.431
Picture many cats, one after another, and keep

00:00:37.472 --> 00:00:39.014
a running average of their visits.

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:42.564
This run starts two, two, three, so the

00:00:42.626 --> 00:00:43.728
average is high at first.

00:00:44.312 --> 00:00:45.865
After ten cats it's two point one.

00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:48.482
After a thousand cats, this run has settled

00:00:48.523 --> 00:00:49.649
at about one point seven.

00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:53.065
Another run would wobble differently, but long runs

00:00:53.207 --> 00:00:54.512
settle near the same centre.

00:00:55.277 --> 00:00:57.873
So the expected value is the long-run average

00:00:58.055 --> 00:00:58.155
of X.

00:00:59.445 --> 00:01:01.135
No cat makes one point seven visits.

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:03.194
It isn't a prediction for one cat.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.847
It's the centre the results settle around.

00:01:06.491 --> 00:01:08.378
We write it as E of X, or

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:10.406
the Greek letter mu, and it's a measure

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:11.832
of centre for the distribution.

00:01:12.436 --> 00:01:14.270
Where does one point seven come from?

00:01:14.996 --> 00:01:17.121
For a fair die, every value has the

00:01:17.163 --> 00:01:19.589
same weight, so E of X is just

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.936
the ordinary average, twenty-one over six.

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:24.348
The cat visits aren't uniform.

00:01:24.972 --> 00:01:26.238
Imagine a thousand cats.

00:01:26.781 --> 00:01:29.225
About a hundred make no visits, three hundred

00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:31.871
make one, four hundred make two and two

00:01:31.932 --> 00:01:32.813
hundred make three.

00:01:33.335 --> 00:01:36.383
That's seventeen hundred visits, so the average is

00:01:36.484 --> 00:01:37.165
one point seven.

00:01:37.989 --> 00:01:40.675
Dividing each count by a thousand gives its

00:01:40.756 --> 00:01:41.397
probability.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:43.988
So the expected value is the sum of

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.098
each value times its probability.

00:01:46.802 --> 00:01:49.047
And the beam balances at one point seven,

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:51.052
pulled towards the heavier bars.

00:01:51.743 --> 00:01:52.528
Now, spread.

00:01:53.152 --> 00:01:55.336
A natural idea is to average how far

00:01:55.436 --> 00:01:57.881
each value is from mu, keeping the sign.

00:01:58.603 --> 00:02:01.812
These signed deviations are minus one point seven,

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:03.517
minus zero point seven,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.105
zero point three and one point three.

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:09.446
Weight each one by its probability, and add.

00:02:10.071 --> 00:02:11.159
You get exactly zero.

00:02:12.652 --> 00:02:13.418
That's not bad luck.

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:16.389
Mu is the balance point, so the weighted

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.018
deviations on the left always cancel the ones

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:19.641
on the right.

00:02:20.705 --> 00:02:23.695
So we square each deviation first, which makes

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:25.561
none of them negative, and then take the

00:02:25.602 --> 00:02:26.304
weighted average.

00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:27.721
That's the variance.

00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:31.313
It's an expected value too: the expected value

00:02:31.415 --> 00:02:33.358
of the squared deviation from mu.

00:02:34.163 --> 00:02:35.196
Here it is for the cats.

00:02:35.781 --> 00:02:38.126
The squared deviations are two point eight nine,

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:41.091
zero point four nine, zero point zero nine

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:42.835
and one point six nine.

00:02:43.417 --> 00:02:45.668
Weight them and add, and the variance is

00:02:45.729 --> 00:02:46.875
zero point eight one.

00:02:47.498 --> 00:02:49.033
But squaring changed the units.

00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:52.226
X counts visits, so the variance is in

00:02:52.446 --> 00:02:53.289
visits squared.

00:02:53.912 --> 00:02:54.898
So take the square root.

00:02:55.523 --> 00:02:58.233
The standard deviation, sigma, is the square root

00:02:58.294 --> 00:03:00.421
of zero point eight one, which is zero

00:03:00.462 --> 00:03:00.903
point nine.

00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:03.555
It measures the spread, in visits.

00:03:04.359 --> 00:03:06.725
Here, the values within one sigma of one

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:09.052
point seven, from zero point eight to two

00:03:09.093 --> 00:03:11.199
point six, carry most of the probability.

00:03:11.862 --> 00:03:13.615
There's a faster way to get the same

00:03:13.655 --> 00:03:13.897
number.

00:03:14.581 --> 00:03:15.364
Expand the square:

00:03:16.046 --> 00:03:19.236
x minus mu, squared, is x squared, minus

00:03:19.296 --> 00:03:21.262
two mu x, plus mu squared.

00:03:22.065 --> 00:03:23.513
Weight each part and add.

00:03:24.216 --> 00:03:25.821
The middle part has the sum of x

00:03:25.881 --> 00:03:27.647
times P of x, which is mu.

00:03:28.451 --> 00:03:30.423
The last has the sum of the probabilities,

00:03:30.604 --> 00:03:31.108
which is one.

00:03:31.872 --> 00:03:34.621
What's left is E of X squared, the

00:03:34.762 --> 00:03:37.632
average of the squared values, minus mu squared.

00:03:38.475 --> 00:03:40.939
For our distribution, E of X squared is

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:41.861
three point seven,

00:03:42.443 --> 00:03:44.847
and three point seven minus one point seven

00:03:44.907 --> 00:03:47.212
squared is zero point eight one,

00:03:47.773 --> 00:03:48.334
the same answer.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:49.787
Be careful.

00:03:50.498 --> 00:03:53.187
E of X squared squares the values, not

00:03:53.208 --> 00:03:55.495
the probabilities, and it isn't the same as

00:03:55.577 --> 00:03:56.218
mu squared.

00:03:56.681 --> 00:03:58.090
Here's how these numbers get used.

00:03:58.752 --> 00:04:00.958
A pet insurer charges three hundred dollars a

00:04:01.018 --> 00:04:02.060
year to cover a cat.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.585
In a year, there's a zero point zero

00:04:04.645 --> 00:04:07.028
two chance of paying an eight thousand dollar

00:04:07.048 --> 00:04:09.812
claim, a zero point zero five chance of

00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:12.475
paying a one thousand dollar claim, and otherwise

00:04:12.795 --> 00:04:13.116
nothing.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:16.685
The expected payout is eight thousand times zero

00:04:16.726 --> 00:04:19.312
point zero two, plus one thousand times zero

00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:21.438
point zero five, which is two hundred and

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:22.080
ten dollars.

00:04:22.803 --> 00:04:25.192
On the beam, that small weight far out

00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:27.160
at eight thousand does most of the pulling.

00:04:27.843 --> 00:04:30.089
So in the long run, the premium beats

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.577
the expected claims by ninety dollars a policy,

00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:34.442
before the insurer's other costs.

00:04:35.164 --> 00:04:37.553
But the standard deviation of the payout is

00:04:37.594 --> 00:04:40.123
about one thousand one hundred and thirty-four dollars.

00:04:40.968 --> 00:04:43.341
One policy swings far more than ninety dollars

00:04:43.423 --> 00:04:43.844
either way.

00:04:44.549 --> 00:04:47.715
That's why insurers need many independent policies, so

00:04:47.916 --> 00:04:49.478
the long-run average takes over.

00:04:50.121 --> 00:04:52.170
Here's a way to remember the quicker formula.

00:04:52.913 --> 00:04:55.618
Mean of the squares, minus the square of

00:04:55.658 --> 00:04:55.978
the mean.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:57.522
Square the values,

00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:00.512
average them with the probabilities, and then subtract

00:05:00.693 --> 00:05:01.415
mu squared.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.736
The answer can never be negative.

00:05:06.493 --> 00:05:06.899
Your turn.

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:10.116
Find E of X and the variance of

00:05:10.318 --> 00:05:11.601
X for this distribution.

00:05:12.163 --> 00:05:13.145
Pause here to answer.

00:05:23.774 --> 00:05:26.339
E of X is zero, plus zero point

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.385
three, plus one, plus zero point three, which

00:05:29.466 --> 00:05:30.447
is one point six.

00:05:31.209 --> 00:05:33.430
E of X squared is zero point three,

00:05:33.551 --> 00:05:36.113
plus two, plus zero point nine, which is

00:05:36.174 --> 00:05:36.994
three point two.

00:05:37.575 --> 00:05:39.904
So the variance is three point two minus

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:42.454
one point six squared, which is zero point

00:05:42.495 --> 00:05:43.036
six four.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.609
Three point two minus one point six is

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:45.991
wrong.

00:05:46.415 --> 00:05:48.389
You subtract mu squared, not mu.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:50.554
Last one.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.005
X takes the values one, two and three.

00:05:54.988 --> 00:05:56.171
Find a and b.

00:05:57.038 --> 00:05:57.844
Pause here to answer.

00:06:08.572 --> 00:06:09.602
Use both facts.

00:06:10.209 --> 00:06:12.795
The probabilities add to one, so a plus

00:06:12.836 --> 00:06:14.159
b is zero point eight.

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:17.021
And the expected value gives zero point two,

00:06:17.203 --> 00:06:19.843
plus two a, plus three b, equals two

00:06:19.884 --> 00:06:22.365
point three, so two a plus three b

00:06:22.546 --> 00:06:23.425
is two point one.

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:26.732
Take away twice the first equation, and b

00:06:26.873 --> 00:06:29.478
is zero point five, so a is zero

00:06:29.519 --> 00:06:29.999
point three.

00:06:31.145 --> 00:06:33.018
A and b both zero point four is

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:33.421
wrong.

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:36.337
It uses only one fact, and its expected

00:06:36.378 --> 00:06:37.624
value is two point two.

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:41.859
So the expected value is the balance point,

00:06:42.302 --> 00:06:43.366
and the long-run average.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.075
The variance is the expected squared distance from

00:06:47.157 --> 00:06:47.216
it,

00:06:47.858 --> 00:06:49.981
and sigma brings it back to the units

00:06:50.103 --> 00:06:50.542
of X.

00:06:51.566 --> 00:06:53.673
Every sum here ran over a list of

00:06:53.854 --> 00:06:54.636
separate values.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.685
Next, a new question.

00:06:57.589 --> 00:06:59.774
What if X can be any value in

00:06:59.816 --> 00:07:02.322
an interval, like a sprinter's reaction time?

00:07:03.227 --> 00:07:05.818
Then the bars get thinner and thinner, and

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:07.104
there's no list to add up.

00:07:09.768 --> 00:07:10.570
Here are the key notes.

00:07:11.214 --> 00:07:12.941
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:13.003 --> 00:07:13.484
to copy them.
